"executive_summary": "This study investigates the global molecular changes in murine macrophage RAW 264.7 cells upon activation with CpG DNA and lipopolysaccharide (LPS) using Attenuated Total Reflectance Fourier Transform Infrared (ATR-FTIR) spectroscopy. The researchers aimed to determine if these immune activators induce detectable spectral shifts indicative of specific metabolic pathways. Using Principal Component Analysis (PCA), the study identified that CpG DNA and LPS treatments significantly increase total nucleic acid concentrations starting from early time points post-activation. Specifically, DNA synthesis follows RNA synthesis, with RNA-specific peaks indicating the activation state within the first minutes of treatment. Both stimuli cause an initial rapid increase in total protein concentration that levels off by two hours. Furthermore, both groups show increased concentrations of fatty acids, triglycerides, and cholesterol, suggesting a shared synthesis pathway and de novo lipogenesis. The study concludes that FTIR spectroscopy serves as an independent modality to monitor macrophage activation dynamics, offering a non-destructive, high-throughput alternative to complex omics techniques that require extensive sample preparation. The findings provide a broad view of relative metabolite amounts, including nucleic acids, lipids, and proteins, which are not readily accessible by other methods.",
